This article is the second in a series based on data collected from 16 interviews conducted with civic, humanitarian, and political experts in Kyiv, Ukraine, from April 13–24, 2026. The goal of the project was to obtain an accurate, up-to-date snapshot of the numerous realities that comprise modern-day Ukraine. This piece examines what victory means for Ukrainians four and a half years after Russia’s full-scale invasion of their country.&nbsp;



Individual participant views do not necessarily reflect the opinions of their respective organizations.&nbsp;



Executive Summary:




Years of loss and military deadlock have broadened Ukrainians’ understanding of victory beyond the original goal of a military success that restores Ukraine’s legal 1991 borders. 



